1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,026
Hi! This is Seoul's '이태원' [i-tae-won],

2
00:00:02,026 --> 00:00:05,845
where cultures from around the world meet. Today,

3
00:00:05,845 --> 00:00:09,820
let's learn to handle sudden emergencies in Korean!

4
00:00:13,410 --> 00:00:14,793
While traveling,

5
00:00:14,793 --> 00:00:19,895
you might suddenly feel sick or '잃어버리다' [i-reo-beo-ri-da] something precious.

6
00:00:19,895 --> 00:00:23,440
The most important thing is to stay '침착' [chim-chak].

7
00:00:27,060 --> 00:00:32,241
Remember: never be shy to say '도와주세요' [do-wa-ju-se-yo] in an emergency.

8
00:00:32,241 --> 00:00:34,640
Koreans are glad to help.

9
00:00:38,980 --> 00:00:42,907
When you feel unwell: '아프다' [a-peu-da] (to be sick),

10
00:00:42,907 --> 00:00:45,205
a descriptive adjective.

11
00:00:45,205 --> 00:00:50,090
Put the spot in front: '배가 아파요' [bae-ga a-pa-yo] — my stomach hurts.

12
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:59,769
If you fall or bump into something: '다치다' [da-chi-da] (to get hurt),

13
00:00:59,769 --> 00:01:03,600
a verb. '다리를 다쳤어요' [da-ri-reul da-chyeo-sseo-yo] — I hurt my leg.

14
00:01:06,550 --> 00:01:08,348
For mild symptoms,

15
00:01:08,348 --> 00:01:12,244
find a '약국' [yak-kkuk] — look for the green cross.

16
00:01:12,244 --> 00:01:15,840
Just say '약 주세요' [yak ju-se-yo] — medicine, please.

17
00:01:19,140 --> 00:01:22,436
If it's serious, go to a '병원' [byeong-won].

18
00:01:22,436 --> 00:01:27,490
Say '병원에 가고 싶어요' [byeong-wo-ne ga-go si-peo-yo] — I want to go to a hospital.

19
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:35,302
For fire or an ambulance, call '119'.

20
00:01:35,302 --> 00:01:37,710
Call and say '도와주세요' [do-wa-ju-se-yo].

21
00:01:43,290 --> 00:01:48,507
For crime or danger, call '112', the police.

22
00:01:48,507 --> 00:01:53,250
Both '112' and '119' are free, 24 hours.

23
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:01,018
When something's gone: '잃어버리다' [i-reo-beo-ri-da] (to lose),

24
00:02:01,018 --> 00:02:04,160
a verb — and today's key expression.

25
00:02:07,900 --> 00:02:16,130
The most reassuring phrase: '도와주세요' [do-wa-ju-se-yo] — a polite request from '돕다' [dop-tta] (to help).

26
00:02:20,810 --> 00:02:25,324
Today's core pattern: '___을 잃어버렸어요' [eul i-reo-beo-ryeo-sseo-yo] [_-_-_-eul-il-eo-beo-ryeot-eo-yo] (I lost ___).

27
00:02:25,324 --> 00:02:28,180
Put the lost item in the blank.

28
00:02:28,770 --> 00:02:33,754
'지갑을 잃어버렸어요' [ji-ga-beul i-reo-beo-ryeo-sseo-yo] — I lost my wallet.

29
00:02:33,754 --> 00:02:37,960
Swap the item: '여권' [yeo-gwon], '휴대폰' [hyu-dae-pon].

30
00:02:38,410 --> 00:02:43,535
Add '도와주세요' [do-wa-ju-se-yo] up front for urgency: '도와주세요,

31
00:02:43,535 --> 00:02:47,910
가방을 잃어버렸어요'! — Help, I lost my bag!

32
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:51,854
They'll ask '어디에서 잃어버렸어요' [eo-di-e-seo i-reo-beo-ryeo-sseo-yo]?

33
00:02:51,854 --> 00:02:54,544
— Where did you lose it?

34
00:02:54,544 --> 00:02:58,130
Answer '지하철에서요' [ji-ha-cheo-re-seo-yo] — on the subway.

35
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:05,879
'배가 아파요' [bae-ga a-pa-yo] — my stomach hurts; '다리를 다쳤어요' [da-ri-reul da-chyeo-sseo-yo] — I hurt my leg.

36
00:03:05,879 --> 00:03:08,420
Point to where it hurts.

37
00:03:11,200 --> 00:03:15,115
Be polite. Start with '실례합니다' [sil-lye-ham-ni-da] (excuse me),

38
00:03:15,115 --> 00:03:18,470
and even strangers will gladly help.

39
00:03:20,770 --> 00:03:23,075
When you report, two keys: what happened,

40
00:03:23,075 --> 00:03:25,380
and where. Just be clear about those two.

41
00:03:33,940 --> 00:03:37,781
They'll ask '무슨 일이에요' [mu-seun i-ri-e-yo]? — What happened?

42
00:03:37,781 --> 00:03:39,750
Just explain calmly.

43
00:03:42,090 --> 00:03:47,994
Call and say '119죠 친구가 다쳤어요 여기는 이태원이에요' — Is this 119?

44
00:03:47,994 --> 00:03:51,930
My friend is hurt. We're in Itaewon.

45
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:58,330
Call and say '112죠 가방을 잃어버렸어요 도와주세요' — Is this 112?

46
00:03:58,330 --> 00:04:01,480
I lost my bag. Please help.

47
00:04:01,930 --> 00:04:04,082
Even in a rush, go slow.

48
00:04:04,082 --> 00:04:09,461
If they don't understand: '다시 말해 주세요' [da-si mal-hae ju-se-yo] — please say it again.

49
00:04:09,461 --> 00:04:14,750
Give your spot: '여기는 이태원역 근처예요' [yeo-gi-neun i-tae-wo-nyeok geun-cheo-ye-yo] — I'm near Itaewon Station.

50
00:04:15,200 --> 00:04:19,575
Now a roleplay. At the pharmacy, say '배가 아파요' [bae-ga a-pa-yo],

51
00:04:19,575 --> 00:04:24,234
'소화제 주세요' [so-hwa-je ju-se-yo] — My stomach hurts; digestive medicine,

52
00:04:24,234 --> 00:04:24,900
please.

53
00:04:27,990 --> 00:04:33,048
At the lost-and-found: '가방을 잃어버렸어요. 도와주세요' [ga-bang-eul i-reo-beo-ryeo-sseo-yo do-wa-ju-se-yo].

54
00:04:33,048 --> 00:04:36,460
— I lost my bag. Please help.

55
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:42,551
In Korea, helping in a crisis is natural.

56
00:04:42,551 --> 00:04:46,640
'112' and '119' even offer interpreter help.

57
00:04:50,160 --> 00:04:53,549
After help, say '고맙습니다' [go-map-sseum-ni-da] — thank you.

58
00:04:53,549 --> 00:04:56,090
A small thanks means a lot.

59
00:05:00,020 --> 00:05:06,010
Today we learned '아프다' [a-peu-da], '다치다' [da-chi-da], '잃어버리다' [i-reo-beo-ri-da],

60
00:05:06,010 --> 00:05:13,074
asked for help with '___을 잃어버렸어요' [eul i-reo-beo-ryeo-sseo-yo] [_-_-_-eul-il-eo-beo-ryeot-eo-yo] and '도와주세요' [do-wa-ju-se-yo],

61
00:05:13,074 --> 00:05:18,910
and practiced calling '112' and '119'.

62
00:05:24,180 --> 00:05:26,090
What emergencies have you faced?

63
00:05:26,090 --> 00:05:27,523
Tell us in the comments.

64
00:05:27,523 --> 00:05:32,120
Let's meet again for helpful Korean — and don't forget to subscribe and like!

